<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Network security configuration (Phase 6).

    Defense in depth: cleartext traffic is already rejected above the
    transport layer — EndpointValidator only accepts HTTP for loopback
    hosts (with a warning) and the OkHttp client never follows redirects
    (so an Authorization header can never leak to a redirect target).
    This config hardens the platform default (cleartext is already
    blocked by default on API 28+, BYOKAI's minSdk) so that no future
    code path can silently open a cleartext connection.

    Interaction with EndpointValidator: with cleartextTrafficPermitted
    set to false here, the validator's loopback-HTTP carve-out is
    unreachable on-device — the platform blocks it at the socket layer
    before any warning could matter. That is the intended outcome: a
    local dev server must be reached over TLS (or tested via the
    validator's unit tests, which do not go through this config).

    No certificate pinning, deliberately: BYOKAI is bring-your-own-key
    with user-supplied custom endpoints (self-hosted gateways, proxies).
    Pinning would break legitimate user endpoints and cannot cover
    endpoints the app has never seen.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" />
</network-security-config>
